WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.040
Okay, I'm going to talk about effective agents.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.940
And what I really mean is I'm going to talk about what it takes for you to build something

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.200
like an agentic AI system.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.879
And again, whatever I mean by that, I mean, there's so much AI in the world, I'm talking

00:00:23.879 --> 00:00:28.760
about the kind of thing where you have a conversational interface, and some AI agent will do something

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:29.760
on your behalf.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.279
And then maybe you'll have a follow-up conversation, and you'll continue to direct the AI to do

00:00:35.279 --> 00:00:36.279
things.

00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:39.680
Think cloud code, think co-pilot chat, that kind of stuff.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.840
If you want to make your own one of them, this is the talk for you.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.000
And the reason I know about this is because I work for Posit, and we've been working on

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.660
Posit Assistant, which is an AI assistant that's now available in our studio.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.619
So we now have the mythical fifth pane on the left-hand side.

00:00:55.619 --> 00:00:57.700
You can talk to Posit Assistant.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.180
It can run our code for you.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.439
The assistant can see your plots, and you can do the kind of things that you can do

00:01:03.439 --> 00:01:07.580
with something like cloud code, but in our studio, and it's really designed to work well

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.220
with the kind of workflows that you will be doing in something like our studio.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.660
I'm talking exploratory data analysis, creating reports, creating dashboards.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.339
We've really thought very carefully about how to create an AI assistant to help with

00:01:20.339 --> 00:01:21.379
that kind of work.

00:01:21.379 --> 00:01:26.059
And usually what that actually means is that a lot of the time we thought, when should

00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:28.379
the agent bring back a human in the loop?

00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:30.580
That's kind of what we've been thinking about.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:35.660
So not async agents, which we've talked about today, not one shot back and forth, but a

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.300
conversational AI agent.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.099
Posit Assistant is also available in Positron.

00:01:41.099 --> 00:01:46.779
Positron actually had an older agent, Positron Assistant, for a little while now.

00:01:46.779 --> 00:01:50.940
If you haven't used Positron's AI stuff in a while, go back, try it again, because we've

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.940
rebuilt the entire thing from scratch.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.820
And that's kind of what this talk is.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:59.459
We rebuilt the thing from scratch, and I'm going to tell you everything we learned in

00:01:59.459 --> 00:02:01.419
the process of doing that.

00:02:01.419 --> 00:02:03.620
So you want to build your own AI agent.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:04.940
How does one do so?

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.379
You could do this.

00:02:06.379 --> 00:02:08.300
You could use an agent SDK.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.179
Now I'm assuming for the rest of the talk that you're not doing something like self-hosted

00:02:13.179 --> 00:02:14.179
models.

00:02:14.179 --> 00:02:16.380
You're using frontier models on a provided service.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.860
You have some kind of platform already set up with guardrails or whatever else you need.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.619
You have access to a good model.

00:02:21.619 --> 00:02:23.580
That might be eventually hitting open AI.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.539
It might eventually be hitting Anthropic.

00:02:25.539 --> 00:02:29.860
But that's kind of the agents that I'm talking, the LLMs that I'm talking about.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.699
Now, if you decide to use these tools, there are some good parts and bad parts.

00:02:33.699 --> 00:02:36.179
The good part is that's very easy.

00:02:36.179 --> 00:02:38.139
You get an AI agent ready to use.

00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:39.179
It's got tools built in.

00:02:39.179 --> 00:02:40.740
It knows how to do things.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:41.779
It's easy to set up.

00:02:41.779 --> 00:02:44.699
It's easy to integrate into other things if you're an engineer.

00:02:44.699 --> 00:02:49.419
But the downside is you're now locked into Microsoft or Anthropic, depending on which

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:50.419
SDK you use.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.460
In the end, these kind of tools, they're not that popular.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.580
You can use them, but you're kind of locked in.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.580
They're also hard to customize.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:02.300
Obviously, this is important for Positive Assistant because we want a very customized

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.259
data science experience.

00:03:04.259 --> 00:03:10.020
So we can't use an off-the-shelf SDK where we can't modify the tools or the tool loop.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.300
So instead, we've decided to build our own.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.619
And there are a few ways you can do this if you want to do this yourself.

00:03:14.619 --> 00:03:16.660
If you're an R user, you can use Elmer.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.059
If you're a Python user, you can use Chatless.

00:03:19.059 --> 00:03:22.580
We built Positive Assistant in a programming language called TypeScript.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.259
It's similar to JavaScript.

00:03:24.259 --> 00:03:26.300
And we use the Vercel AI SDK.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:31.339
And what these do is they let you talk to model providers in a standardized way.

00:03:31.339 --> 00:03:35.660
So you can talk to Anthropic or OpenAI or Google, and you can use the same primitives

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:39.139
as you would over all the various different providers.

00:03:39.139 --> 00:03:42.940
And it means that you don't need to know the specifics of how to talk to each provider.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.580
Now, despite that, I'm going to take some time to talk about how you would do that.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:53.220
I want to talk about some fundamentals of how you actually talk to an LLM via a Frontier

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.220
provided service.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:59.100
And the reason I want to do that is because everything you need to know about creating

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.539
an AI agent that works autonomously all comes down to these fundamentals in the end.

00:04:03.539 --> 00:04:04.699
It's not magic.

00:04:04.699 --> 00:04:08.580
It's repeated loops of talking to an LLM provider.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.380
For a while, we're going to get a little technical, and I'm going to talk about what happens when

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.660
you type hello world into a box and you hit the send button, what does it look like?

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:19.739
So this is JSON way of formatting data.

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:24.779
And when you talk to one of these AI models, you're sending formatted data over the network,

00:04:24.779 --> 00:04:29.299
over the internet to their provider service, potentially via some kind of guardrails or

00:04:29.299 --> 00:04:30.779
proxy service and stuff.

00:04:30.779 --> 00:04:35.459
But at the end of the day, it's going to hit Anthropic or OpenAI or Google.

00:04:35.459 --> 00:04:39.459
And here I'm using what you would send if you were talking to Anthropic.

00:04:39.459 --> 00:04:43.339
I'm skipping some bits and pieces, simplifying, but it essentially looks like this.

00:04:43.339 --> 00:04:48.619
You choose a model, you set a system prompt that controls the behavior of your agent,

00:04:48.619 --> 00:04:51.459
and you send a user message, what the user said.

00:04:51.459 --> 00:04:55.619
Now, when you do that, you will get a response back and the response will look like this.

00:04:55.619 --> 00:04:59.619
Again, I don't want you to kind of worry about all of this stuff.

00:04:59.619 --> 00:05:02.299
What I want you to pay attention to is this content block.

00:05:02.299 --> 00:05:06.380
You'll get back some content from the assistant that looks like that.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.059
Now this could be streamed in.

00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:13.100
Again, there's complications, there's IDs, there's sort of stop reasons and usage information.

00:05:13.339 --> 00:05:16.059
Fundamentally, you're getting a response from the assistant.

00:05:16.059 --> 00:05:19.820
And then that's kind of the rest of what we're doing is just back and forth,

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:23.339
requests and responses, requests and responses with additional bits tacked on.

00:05:23.339 --> 00:05:29.019
Now, if in the old days you use web APIs before the advent of LLMs, you might think,

00:05:29.019 --> 00:05:30.459
okay, this is a conversation.

00:05:30.459 --> 00:05:31.820
I want to continue that conversation.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:32.940
I want to ask another question.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:33.660
How do I do that?

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.619
And you might think, oh, well, maybe I just pass this ID and I pass another user message.

00:05:38.619 --> 00:05:40.299
And it turns out that's not the case.

00:05:40.299 --> 00:05:44.940
The fundamental thing you need to remember when working with AIs at this level is that

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:47.660
you're sending the conversation over and over again.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:54.059
The entire history of that conversation gets sent every time the assistant makes a reply.

00:05:54.059 --> 00:05:55.579
And that's kind of weird.

00:05:55.579 --> 00:05:59.100
It's a bit different to how a lot of web services used to work,

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.899
where you'd have something like a cookie or a session to track things over time.

00:06:03.899 --> 00:06:06.459
No, you send the conversation every time.

00:06:06.459 --> 00:06:09.660
And then two things should immediately flag to you when I tell you that.

00:06:09.739 --> 00:06:13.739
One, if you have a long running agent or a long conversation,

00:06:13.739 --> 00:06:16.859
surely this gets very long and you have to send a lot of data.

00:06:16.859 --> 00:06:17.339
Yes.

00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:20.459
And the other thing that should shout out to you is that when you do that,

00:06:20.459 --> 00:06:26.220
after a while, you are paying for all of these tokens again and again and again.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:29.019
And every time that someone sends another message,

00:06:29.019 --> 00:06:31.260
you get another message at the bottom of the list.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.579
It's an append-only data structure if you're having a single linear conversation.

00:06:35.579 --> 00:06:39.179
And all that's going to happen is right at the end, you're just going to change this part.

00:06:39.179 --> 00:06:41.579
And everything else is going to stay the same.

00:06:41.579 --> 00:06:42.779
Well, you pay for it all anyway.

00:06:42.779 --> 00:06:44.140
You pay for it again and again.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:46.619
That's going to become more important later when I talk about caching.

00:06:46.619 --> 00:06:50.220
When you're using an AI agent, it has a way to interact with the world.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.420
We call them tools.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.179
A tool description looks like this.

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:56.220
You have a name, you've got some text that describes it to the model,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.980
and then a way to tell the LLM how to invoke this tool.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:02.700
And again, if you've used something like Elmer or ChatList before,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.260
any of these tools that exist for languages, you know all this.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.980
You just do it in a different way, a much nicer format.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.179
So a full request with tool loops, tool use block here.

00:07:11.179 --> 00:07:13.260
The model is opening a file.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.820
That response comes back to the model, back and forth.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:16.859
Kind of looks like this.

00:07:16.859 --> 00:07:19.500
Now, I'm not going to make the rest of the talk full of JSON blocks

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:20.940
because I can't make you look at that any longer.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.299
I'm going to use a schematic view.

00:07:22.299 --> 00:07:26.700
And what I want you to just get an idea for is what we call the agentic loop.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.420
This is the lifeblood of an AI agent.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:30.859
You have some kind of setup.

00:07:30.859 --> 00:07:32.220
You have a user turn.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.739
And then each user turn is prompted by someone has sat,

00:07:35.739 --> 00:07:37.500
typed a question in a box and pressed enter.

00:07:37.579 --> 00:07:38.540
That's a turn.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:43.260
Now, because inside a single turn, an assistant might use some tools,

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:45.260
every time the assistant uses a tool,

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.140
barring things like parallel tool calls and various subtleties,

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:51.899
but generally every time a tool is used, that's a single back and forth

00:07:51.899 --> 00:07:55.019
because a request has come back to say, please use this tool.

00:07:55.019 --> 00:07:57.019
And the value of the response has gone back to the model.

00:07:57.019 --> 00:07:58.540
So back and forth and back and forth.

00:07:58.540 --> 00:07:59.500
And that's the loop.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:00.619
Read the assistant message.

00:08:00.619 --> 00:08:01.739
We check for tool calls.

00:08:01.739 --> 00:08:02.619
We run the tool calls.

00:08:02.619 --> 00:08:03.339
We send them back.

00:08:03.339 --> 00:08:04.619
We check the assistant message.

00:08:04.619 --> 00:08:05.739
We check if there's tool calls.

00:08:05.739 --> 00:08:06.619
We run the tool calls.

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:07.579
We send it back.

00:08:07.579 --> 00:08:09.260
And we do it over and over again.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:10.859
That's the agentic loop.

00:08:10.859 --> 00:08:13.179
And then eventually the assistant will stop and it'll say,

00:08:13.179 --> 00:08:14.220
okay, I understand.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.260
Here's your answer.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:16.859
And then we wait for the next turn.

00:08:16.859 --> 00:08:18.619
And that's how things like Posit Assistant work,

00:08:18.619 --> 00:08:21.420
that's how Claude Code works and Copilot Chat.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:25.820
So now that we're all experts on fundamental messaging APIs,

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.779
I'm now going to talk about some of the learnings we had

00:08:28.779 --> 00:08:31.339
building Posit Assistant so that I can share it with you.

00:08:31.339 --> 00:08:34.780
So the first thing I'll say is that Claude is a liar.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.299
He will bare-faced lie to you again and again.

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:40.539
And, you know, we've talked about hallucination.

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:42.780
Hallucination, actually, you know, it's getting better.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:43.580
It still happens.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.940
It's not as bad as it was.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.140
But this is kind of a more subtle problem that still exists.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:51.260
We have a system in Posit Assistant so that when the model

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:53.580
runs a piece of R code that produces a plot,

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:55.179
that plot is sent back to the model.

00:08:55.179 --> 00:08:58.140
So the model can view that plot using a system called Vision.

00:08:58.140 --> 00:09:01.659
And most frontier LLMs can handle images these days.

00:09:01.659 --> 00:09:03.580
But on several occasions during development,

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:06.619
we've broken this feature and we didn't realize that we'd

00:09:06.619 --> 00:09:07.580
broken the feature.

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:10.539
And the reason why was because the model is just so,

00:09:10.539 --> 00:09:13.020
just convincing in its responses.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.260
It was like, surely it's not broken.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:15.820
I can see.

00:09:16.619 --> 00:09:17.820
And I'm going to show you an example of this.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.900
I've hooked an agent up into the slide.

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:23.419
So here I've got an example asking the agent to plot

00:09:23.419 --> 00:09:25.739
something, a horsepower mass per gallon,

00:09:25.739 --> 00:09:27.419
famous empty cars data set.

00:09:27.419 --> 00:09:29.260
And I'm going to ask it to describe what it sees.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:30.219
So I've sent that off.

00:09:30.219 --> 00:09:32.700
Okay, so it's trying to run some tools.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.979
It's running a piece of R code and that R code's executed

00:09:35.979 --> 00:09:38.539
and some result images sent back.

00:09:38.539 --> 00:09:41.179
The assistant said the plot shows a clear negative

00:09:41.179 --> 00:09:43.900
relationship between horsepower and fuel efficiency.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:44.859
Okay, brilliant.

00:09:44.859 --> 00:09:45.419
That's right.

00:09:45.419 --> 00:09:48.140
That's doing its job, except watch this.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:53.260
Yeah, what I did is I made it so that literally the word image

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:54.940
is returned, not the image data.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.979
And it took me to ask, ask the model, you know,

00:09:57.979 --> 00:09:59.179
did you really see that plot?

00:09:59.179 --> 00:10:02.940
No, it kind of just used what it expected to see

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.219
because the empty cars data set is such a well-known data set.

00:10:06.219 --> 00:10:07.580
It knew what the plot would look like.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:08.940
And that's what it described.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.380
Now I knew this was going to happen.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.219
Your users might not know that's going to happen.

00:10:12.219 --> 00:10:13.020
How would they know?

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:16.780
There could be subtle biases introduced into the data

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.539
because this kind of thing happened.

00:10:18.539 --> 00:10:21.820
So these are something to be aware of that.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:23.419
Yeah, Claude will lie to you.

00:10:23.419 --> 00:10:26.299
And in fact, we at Posit, we've been trying to understand

00:10:26.299 --> 00:10:26.859
how this works.

00:10:26.859 --> 00:10:28.140
We've done some deep dives.

00:10:28.619 --> 00:10:31.099
And what we've really found is that LLMs really do see

00:10:31.099 --> 00:10:31.900
what they expect to see.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.900
And this is a really interesting article.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:37.419
If you're interested in that, Simon and Sarah,

00:10:37.419 --> 00:10:40.619
they take famous data sets and flip them.

00:10:40.619 --> 00:10:41.979
So this is empty cars.

00:10:41.979 --> 00:10:44.219
But here we've got as horsepower goes up,

00:10:44.219 --> 00:10:45.419
the miles per gallon goes up.

00:10:45.419 --> 00:10:46.460
And that's not right.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:47.979
What they've done is they flipped the plot.

00:10:47.979 --> 00:10:49.739
Well, they flipped the data and then plotted it.

00:10:49.739 --> 00:10:51.820
And they send that to the LLM and they ask,

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:53.260
can you describe this plot to me?

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:56.460
And overwhelmingly at the time, what were frontier models

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:57.500
will get this wrong.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.500
They will describe that image wrong.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:01.900
They will say always horsepower goes up,

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.179
miles per gallon goes down

00:11:03.179 --> 00:11:05.260
because that's what they expected to see.

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:08.140
And you can see here that most models failed at this task.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:09.500
So yeah, do keep that in mind.

00:11:10.299 --> 00:11:13.099
Always, always, always, if you're building AI agents,

00:11:13.099 --> 00:11:15.179
check the raw messages, check the network.

00:11:16.219 --> 00:11:19.260
Don't just ask the LLM why it did something.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:20.940
Like, oh, why did you do this?

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:21.659
Why did you do that?

00:11:21.659 --> 00:11:23.659
What were you thinking when you returned this?

00:11:23.659 --> 00:11:25.580
LLMs are really bad at introspection.

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:29.739
They are not good at analyzing their own conversation history.

00:11:29.739 --> 00:11:32.940
It's actually possible to sort of forge that history,

00:11:34.299 --> 00:11:36.700
because you're sending the conversation history every time.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:38.460
You can forge this and make the assistant

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:39.340
say whatever you want.

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:41.260
And then if you ask it, why did you say that?

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:44.460
It will say something that sounds convincing

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:45.580
about why it happened.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:46.859
So you gotta be really careful.

00:11:46.859 --> 00:11:49.419
Don't ask the model why it did something.

00:11:49.419 --> 00:11:51.419
Check the raw data underneath and really see

00:11:51.419 --> 00:11:52.780
if you can figure out what's happened

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:54.460
that's returned some weird result.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:57.739
Top tip, if you can, use a man-in-the-middle proxy.

00:11:57.739 --> 00:11:59.739
If you don't have access to the raw data,

00:11:59.739 --> 00:12:01.820
for whatever reason, and you're doing this

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.900
on a machine you control with admin access,

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.260
then do try out these tools.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:06.380
ProxyMan's a great one.

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:08.940
There's CharlesProxy and some open source stuff.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.099
They'll let you view the raw traffic.

00:12:11.099 --> 00:12:14.140
Even if the debugging information in your app

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.380
isn't good enough, you can use these tools to do so.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:18.539
So what I'll do is I'll really quickly explain

00:12:18.539 --> 00:12:19.900
sort of how these proxies work.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:22.219
So I was to sort of pick two people in the audience

00:12:22.219 --> 00:12:24.539
and say, oh, whisper in that person's ear

00:12:24.539 --> 00:12:25.659
a three-digit number.

00:12:25.659 --> 00:12:26.940
And they did that.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.419
None of us would know what that three-digit number is.

00:12:29.419 --> 00:12:30.059
We just wouldn't know

00:12:30.059 --> 00:12:32.299
because it's sort of this private communication, right?

00:12:32.299 --> 00:12:33.580
That's like the internet.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:36.539
That's how most internet communication happens.

00:12:36.539 --> 00:12:38.780
What I'm going to do is I'm going to go over here.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:40.140
It's 165, yeah.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:41.979
That's what a man-in-the-middle proxy does.

00:12:41.979 --> 00:12:44.460
It watches your traffic and it shares it

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:46.780
so that you can see exactly what's going on.

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:49.099
So you now will know the three-digit number.

00:12:49.099 --> 00:12:50.219
And very similar to that,

00:12:50.219 --> 00:12:52.619
if you set up a man-in-the-middle proxy on your machine,

00:12:52.619 --> 00:12:55.179
you can view all of the raw traffic on, you know,

00:12:55.179 --> 00:12:56.859
any tool you have installed on that machine.

00:12:56.859 --> 00:12:58.299
Like I said, you need admin access.

00:12:58.859 --> 00:13:00.140
A bit of a dangerous thing to do

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:01.099
if you're not being careful

00:13:01.099 --> 00:13:02.140
because you can leak things.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:04.780
But, you know, it's a really, really, really useful tool

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:08.940
for fundamentally seeing what is being sent to an AI model.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.659
Okay, many of the frontier providers,

00:13:12.219 --> 00:13:14.140
not everyone, but most, you know,

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.979
Anthropic and OpenAI certainly,

00:13:15.979 --> 00:13:20.140
will provide deep discounts if you have cached LLM tokens.

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:21.820
What I mean by that is that

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:23.340
if you send the same thing twice

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.979
within some predetermined time period,

00:13:25.979 --> 00:13:29.179
you'll pay a lot less for those tokens the second time.

00:13:29.179 --> 00:13:31.659
OpenAI have a way to do this automatically.

00:13:31.659 --> 00:13:34.700
Anthropic have a manual cache control markers.

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:37.099
You can do it automatically with Anthropic these days,

00:13:37.099 --> 00:13:39.099
but with PositiveSystem,

00:13:39.099 --> 00:13:40.859
we like the manual cache control markers

00:13:40.859 --> 00:13:42.380
because it lets us do clever things

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:44.619
with the MFR context and things like that.

00:13:44.619 --> 00:13:46.539
But yeah, these discounts can be very, very deep,

00:13:46.539 --> 00:13:49.020
like 90% cheaper by using caching.

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:50.219
The way this works is that

00:13:50.219 --> 00:13:52.780
when you send a user request, like here,

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.140
you can attach headers.

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:56.780
This is a cache control header attached to that user message.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:59.260
And, you know, this could return

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:01.820
some kind of like 40, you know, tool calls or whatever.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:03.179
This could be a very long message.

00:14:03.179 --> 00:14:04.859
But next time the user sends a message

00:14:04.859 --> 00:14:05.900
following up from that,

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.059
everything in blue, the model has seen before.

00:14:08.059 --> 00:14:09.659
All of this, all of this has seen before,

00:14:09.659 --> 00:14:11.020
all of this has seen before.

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:13.900
And you only pay full price kind of for the latest,

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:16.380
which if you imagine a very long conversation

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:17.659
with lots and lots of tool calls

00:14:17.659 --> 00:14:19.179
and lots and lots of turns,

00:14:19.179 --> 00:14:22.539
the ratio of the new stuff is very small

00:14:22.539 --> 00:14:25.340
as compared to everything that the model has seen before.

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:27.739
So you can save a lot of money

00:14:27.739 --> 00:14:29.020
for you or your company

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:31.580
by taking advantage of these caching discounts.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.659
With PositiveSystem, we try and achieve,

00:14:33.659 --> 00:14:35.820
you know, greater than 80% cache rate hits.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:37.979
There is a way to view actually

00:14:37.979 --> 00:14:40.539
what your cache hit rate is in PositiveSystem.

00:14:41.099 --> 00:14:42.539
We can make it up to 90%,

00:14:42.539 --> 00:14:44.140
but there are some gotchas.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:46.299
So if you are thinking of using caching,

00:14:46.299 --> 00:14:47.099
some top tips.

00:14:47.099 --> 00:14:48.619
Try not to change the system prompt.

00:14:48.619 --> 00:14:51.419
We used to do a thing where the system prompt

00:14:51.419 --> 00:14:52.619
sent to the model would change

00:14:52.619 --> 00:14:53.580
depending on what you're doing.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:55.179
It was a sort of template system.

00:14:55.179 --> 00:14:56.460
You know, so if you're using R

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.140
and then you flip to a Python console,

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.780
the prompt would change to indicate that.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:02.700
Unfortunately, you'll find that changing the system prompt

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.299
completely invalidates the caching.

00:15:04.299 --> 00:15:06.380
It's changing the very start of the message

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:07.500
sent to the LLM.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:09.419
So you have to pay full price for everything again.

00:15:09.419 --> 00:15:10.460
So as much as possible,

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:12.859
try not to change the history if you can.

00:15:12.859 --> 00:15:14.299
Also, if you are doing that

00:15:14.299 --> 00:15:16.539
and you're not trying to change the history,

00:15:16.539 --> 00:15:19.739
sometimes you can use these XML style tags

00:15:19.739 --> 00:15:20.619
in your user message

00:15:20.619 --> 00:15:22.059
that's kind of injected

00:15:22.059 --> 00:15:23.580
to change the behavior of the model.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:24.940
Cloud Code does this,

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:26.780
or at least it used to the last time I looked.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:29.179
You can change the behavior of the model

00:15:29.179 --> 00:15:31.260
by putting instructions inside XML tags

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:32.299
that look kind of like this.

00:15:32.299 --> 00:15:34.460
And the LLM will listen to those.

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:37.419
It's not as strong as true system prompting,

00:15:37.419 --> 00:15:40.299
but it is enough to change the behavior of the system

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:44.619
without changing your entire history

00:15:44.619 --> 00:15:46.140
by changing the system prompt.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.900
That's true of tools too, by the way.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:50.460
We used to make various tools available or not available

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:52.380
depending on the context in Positron.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.539
But again, if you change which tools are available,

00:15:54.539 --> 00:15:56.059
that completely invalidates the cache

00:15:56.059 --> 00:15:58.219
and you're paying 100% for, you know,

00:15:58.219 --> 00:16:01.179
200,000 tokens, 250,000 tokens on the next turn.

00:16:01.179 --> 00:16:02.299
So you gotta be careful.

00:16:03.419 --> 00:16:05.340
And keep an eye on how long you expect

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:07.500
your user to reply to you.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.179
So if you're writing an AI agent

00:16:09.179 --> 00:16:11.580
and you're expecting a very asynchronous conversation,

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.179
a back and forth within five minutes,

00:16:13.179 --> 00:16:15.020
you can probably get away with the five minutes.

00:16:15.979 --> 00:16:17.979
If you're expecting a user to come back after,

00:16:17.979 --> 00:16:19.580
what, 20 minutes, 40 minutes,

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.059
you might wanna think about using a longer caching header.

00:16:22.059 --> 00:16:23.340
And what's available,

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:24.859
it's gonna depend on your provider.

00:16:24.859 --> 00:16:27.580
Okay, so yeah, so tools are the main way

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:30.299
for LLMs to interact with the outside world.

00:16:30.299 --> 00:16:32.780
They're kind of what makes the AI agent

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:34.619
able to do some work on your behalf.

00:16:34.619 --> 00:16:37.099
Another top tip for you, manage your tool outputs.

00:16:38.219 --> 00:16:40.619
If you have things like a bash tool

00:16:40.619 --> 00:16:42.619
or a tool to run R code,

00:16:42.619 --> 00:16:46.059
don't just naively put the results of that tool

00:16:46.059 --> 00:16:47.580
into your conversation history.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:50.059
Here's an example where this is sort of,

00:16:50.059 --> 00:16:51.179
it's not really data related,

00:16:51.179 --> 00:16:53.419
but some kind of like sysadmin related thing.

00:16:53.419 --> 00:16:55.179
Someone might print some log files

00:16:55.179 --> 00:16:56.059
and if they're not careful,

00:16:56.059 --> 00:16:58.700
you might accidentally get gigabytes of log files

00:16:58.700 --> 00:16:59.979
in your conversation history.

00:16:59.979 --> 00:17:00.940
And that's gonna break things.

00:17:01.979 --> 00:17:03.979
The model's not gonna be able to use

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:07.180
over a million characters of code.

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:08.619
They're not very good at that.

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:10.540
They'd be able to pick out individual things,

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:12.619
but they wouldn't be able to aggregate, for example.

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:13.500
They're not good at that.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:14.699
But not only that,

00:17:14.699 --> 00:17:16.859
but the next time the user says something,

00:17:16.859 --> 00:17:18.459
the whole conversation history is gonna break

00:17:18.459 --> 00:17:19.579
because you've blown up,

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:21.339
you filled the context with this log file

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:23.900
and there's only a finite amount of space

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:24.780
that you can use.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:26.459
So here's a good trick.

00:17:26.459 --> 00:17:28.140
If the output from a tool

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.699
happens to do something like that,

00:17:30.699 --> 00:17:32.060
don't give it straight to the model,

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:34.780
write it to disk in some temporary location

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.420
and give the path to the model instead.

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:38.219
Because these models,

00:17:38.219 --> 00:17:40.780
they're trained to use tools like search tools,

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:42.459
read tools, you know, grepping.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:44.540
They're trained to use these tools

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:46.459
where they can take very long files.

00:17:46.459 --> 00:17:49.819
Now this could be millions of lines of code of log files

00:17:49.819 --> 00:17:51.660
and they're trained to be able to extract,

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:55.020
search for and extract partial parts of that

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:56.380
without putting the whole thing

00:17:56.380 --> 00:17:57.739
in your conversation history.

00:17:57.739 --> 00:17:58.699
So there's something like this.

00:17:58.699 --> 00:18:00.939
Let the model know that it's a truncated output,

00:18:00.939 --> 00:18:02.459
you know, some end lines,

00:18:02.459 --> 00:18:03.739
write to disk somewhere

00:18:03.739 --> 00:18:05.339
and let it know what it should expect.

00:18:05.339 --> 00:18:06.859
That's really useful.

00:18:06.859 --> 00:18:10.459
If you find that you're using a context a lot

00:18:10.459 --> 00:18:12.380
with your AI agentic workflows,

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:16.219
what you can do is use sub-agents.

00:18:16.219 --> 00:18:17.579
Sub-agents are useful

00:18:17.579 --> 00:18:19.180
if you're doing some kind of work

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:23.020
and you don't want to use all of that context history.

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:25.339
You want to use that all up with your agent.

00:18:25.339 --> 00:18:27.500
You want to kind of just send off a sub-agent

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:28.380
to do that work for you.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:31.739
This is useful because what you can do is

00:18:31.739 --> 00:18:34.380
as a tool call, you can create a sub-agent.

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:36.619
You can set up a tool that can create a sub-agent

00:18:36.619 --> 00:18:38.219
and what that will do is your sub-agent

00:18:38.219 --> 00:18:39.739
will have some kind of system prompt

00:18:39.739 --> 00:18:43.099
that might be different from the main agentic loop

00:18:43.739 --> 00:18:46.140
and the user message, rather than written by the user,

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.780
will be written by the model in this tool call.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:50.060
And then this can rip, right?

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:53.660
This could be, you know, 200,000, 300,000 tokens,

00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:54.859
pool calls over and over again,

00:18:54.859 --> 00:18:56.219
40 tool calls, 50 tool calls,

00:18:56.219 --> 00:18:58.780
some kind of long running agentic workflow

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:00.699
could happen all inside this sub-agent.

00:19:00.699 --> 00:19:02.780
But that final message from the assistant

00:19:03.339 --> 00:19:04.540
when the work has been done

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:07.660
can then be taken and then injected back

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:11.180
into the original tool loop as a result from this tool.

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:13.339
So what you've done is you've compressed

00:19:13.339 --> 00:19:15.979
all of this work that happened down

00:19:15.979 --> 00:19:18.459
into a single message of just a few tokens

00:19:18.459 --> 00:19:20.540
and you've put it inside your main agentic loop

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:22.459
and then the assistant can continue

00:19:22.459 --> 00:19:24.140
and it means that you've used up,

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:27.020
you've done a lot of work agentically

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:29.339
without using up your context history

00:19:29.339 --> 00:19:32.140
and it means that the user can continue their conversation,

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:33.500
you know, go in a different direction

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:34.619
and do some other work.

00:19:34.619 --> 00:19:36.859
The game here is to basically try and last

00:19:36.859 --> 00:19:39.339
as long as possible before having to compact.

00:19:39.339 --> 00:19:40.619
That's kind of the game.

00:19:40.619 --> 00:19:44.060
So earlier, Colin talked a lot about feedback loops

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:46.060
and as the future of AI.

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:48.939
And I agree strongly, tools and feedback loops

00:19:48.939 --> 00:19:50.939
ground the model in reality.

00:19:50.939 --> 00:19:54.380
Without it, you know, after a single hallucination,

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:56.459
the model just spins off and you've kind of lost it

00:19:56.459 --> 00:19:58.540
and it does, you know, it goes off the rails.

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:01.260
Feedback loops bring it back into the ground.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:03.500
A lot of things that are becoming popular

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:05.420
in terms of workflows with AI agents,

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:06.699
things like test-driven development,

00:20:06.699 --> 00:20:08.300
again, this is something Colin talked about,

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:10.939
planning and implementation phases,

00:20:10.939 --> 00:20:13.739
using languages with good compilers,

00:20:13.739 --> 00:20:16.140
Rust and TypeScript with good error messages.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:18.219
All of the reasons that these are becoming popular

00:20:18.219 --> 00:20:19.739
in the software engineering world

00:20:19.739 --> 00:20:21.900
is because it grounds the model in reality

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:23.979
and it stops it spinning off in one direction

00:20:23.979 --> 00:20:24.859
and getting lost.

00:20:24.859 --> 00:20:27.500
The same thing happens in kind of,

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:29.579
not necessarily software development,

00:20:29.819 --> 00:20:32.619
but other orchestration and AI agent flows.

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:35.900
What we've gone for in particular with Posit Assistant

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:37.420
is when we know that the user's doing

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:39.739
some kind of data exploration,

00:20:40.300 --> 00:20:42.140
we kind of trust the model less

00:20:42.140 --> 00:20:44.459
and bring the human into the loop more.

00:20:44.459 --> 00:20:47.420
And that is, you know, it's not as deterministic as this,

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:49.579
but it's still a way of grounding the model.

00:20:49.579 --> 00:20:51.500
So if you are writing AI agents,

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:52.859
what I want you to think about

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:56.540
is in your workflow or your pipeline

00:20:57.500 --> 00:21:00.619
or whatever you're building your AI agent to do,

00:21:00.619 --> 00:21:02.619
what parts of that workflow

00:21:02.619 --> 00:21:06.219
can be independently automated deterministically?

00:21:06.219 --> 00:21:08.380
What can be verified or tested?

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:10.699
Think about that, turn it into tools,

00:21:10.699 --> 00:21:13.020
because then the model can use those tools

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:14.219
to stop it spinning off.

00:21:14.219 --> 00:21:16.300
It keeps it grounded, keeps it on track.

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:17.979
Really think about that the best you can.

00:21:17.979 --> 00:21:19.500
For everything else, if there are things

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:21.819
that you know must be true in your workflow,

00:21:21.819 --> 00:21:23.979
but you can't test for whatever reason,

00:21:23.979 --> 00:21:26.380
think perhaps there's a time

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:27.819
to bring a human into the loop.

00:21:27.819 --> 00:21:29.579
Software engineering is one of these things

00:21:29.579 --> 00:21:31.339
where these tools have become very popular

00:21:31.339 --> 00:21:34.219
because they can run asynchronously and agentically,

00:21:34.219 --> 00:21:36.780
and they can run, you know, for a very long time,

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:38.140
hours in the background.

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:41.500
Maybe that's not the case with your particular work.

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:43.579
Maybe you need to bring a human into the loop more.

00:21:43.579 --> 00:21:45.500
Okay, I will really quickly say

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:47.979
that it's tempting with these tools

00:21:47.979 --> 00:21:49.819
when you're building your own AI agents

00:21:49.819 --> 00:21:52.300
to sort of add in as much customization as possible.

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:54.939
Oh, maybe the user could add this or that.

00:21:54.939 --> 00:21:56.380
Maybe we should have some kind

00:21:56.380 --> 00:22:00.060
of graph-based agentic workflow.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:03.420
Maybe we should have very complex sub-agent definitions,

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.020
things like that, custom instructions

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:06.380
that the user can supply.

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:08.859
What I will say is that that stuff's really nice,

00:22:08.859 --> 00:22:13.339
but do, if you can, instead stick with known protocols

00:22:13.339 --> 00:22:14.939
and standards that already exist.

00:22:14.939 --> 00:22:18.060
We learned that these things are much easier

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:19.260
for the models to pass,

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:21.819
largely because they're trained to do these things.

00:22:21.819 --> 00:22:25.819
Things like agents.md or copilot.md or whatever,

00:22:25.819 --> 00:22:28.060
things like skills that just mark down text.

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:29.900
Models are good at reading text.

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:32.380
Model context protocol, the kind of thing Neil talked about.

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:34.859
Models are trained to be able to use the tools from here.

00:22:34.859 --> 00:22:36.300
Users know how to use those things.

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:38.619
They can bring them in from competing products,

00:22:38.619 --> 00:22:40.859
and you can onboard them onto your AI agent,

00:22:40.859 --> 00:22:43.020
and models already know how to use these things.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:45.180
So try, if you can, if you're thinking

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:46.939
of customizing the agentic loop

00:22:46.939 --> 00:22:49.500
to allow user customization for workflows,

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:52.380
try and do it by introducing a skill, for example,

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:54.780
which will allow you to do things like slash command.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:55.260
Okay.

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:57.339
Agents are trained to be friendly assistants.

00:22:57.339 --> 00:22:58.380
We already saw that earlier,

00:22:58.380 --> 00:22:59.900
where Claude just lied to me

00:22:59.900 --> 00:23:02.699
to be as a friendly, useful person as possible.

00:23:03.979 --> 00:23:06.699
And because of that, they'll work to the end and back

00:23:06.699 --> 00:23:08.780
to try and do the job that you ask for,

00:23:08.780 --> 00:23:10.859
including working around your guardrails.

00:23:10.859 --> 00:23:12.780
Here's an example, fairly recently,

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.099
I think I saw this like maybe yesterday or the day before,

00:23:15.099 --> 00:23:16.859
where someone was working in an environment

00:23:16.859 --> 00:23:18.699
that did not have sudo access.

00:23:18.699 --> 00:23:19.819
They did not have access

00:23:19.819 --> 00:23:21.979
to admin settings on their machine.

00:23:21.979 --> 00:23:24.060
And yet the model saw that,

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:25.579
it saw that there was no sudo,

00:23:25.579 --> 00:23:27.819
and it just went ahead and did it anyway

00:23:27.819 --> 00:23:30.219
by this sort of backdoor that exists

00:23:30.219 --> 00:23:32.939
if you happen to have Docker access on your machine.

00:23:32.939 --> 00:23:34.300
And this is kind of known.

00:23:34.300 --> 00:23:37.260
It's kind of this not very well-spoken about thing

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:39.500
that if you have a Docker access on your machine,

00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:41.260
it's the same as having admin access.

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.540
The model, you'd think, surely the model knows

00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:45.579
that it shouldn't do that,

00:23:45.579 --> 00:23:47.420
that that's kind of wrong, right?

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:48.939
But no, they're being helpful.

00:23:48.939 --> 00:23:51.900
They want to write a file with admin permissions,

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:53.900
so it's going to find a way to do it.

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:57.819
We find this with Posit Assistant,

00:23:57.819 --> 00:23:59.420
if you're trying to choose guardrails,

00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:01.339
like, oh, we'll temporarily turn off access

00:24:01.339 --> 00:24:02.219
to the R console,

00:24:02.219 --> 00:24:04.540
so it can't run code or whatever, right?

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:06.140
Well, it's got access to Bash,

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:08.380
so it'll just start a new R session in Bash

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:09.579
and do it that way instead.

00:24:09.579 --> 00:24:12.459
It doesn't realize that it shouldn't really do that.

00:24:12.459 --> 00:24:14.219
And that takes me to this sort of idea

00:24:14.219 --> 00:24:15.819
of probabilistic debugging.

00:24:15.819 --> 00:24:17.500
So how do we train the models

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:19.260
out of those kinds of behaviors?

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:19.979
It's hard to do.

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:23.339
There is the idea of evals, evaluations,

00:24:23.339 --> 00:24:26.219
so we can measure the behavior of a model

00:24:26.219 --> 00:24:29.500
by putting different scenarios

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:31.500
in front of the model and seeing what it does.

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:33.500
And then we can use a bigger model

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.180
to judge whether that model

00:24:35.180 --> 00:24:36.380
did the right thing or not,

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:39.020
whether it passed or failed some behavioral test.

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:41.180
They're really useful, evals, for debugging.

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:42.780
We use evals to debug exactly

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:44.540
how Posit Assistant behaves.

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:47.260
But I will say this can get very expensive,

00:24:47.260 --> 00:24:49.099
especially when you're using a larger model

00:24:49.099 --> 00:24:50.939
to judge the behavior of a smaller model.

00:24:50.939 --> 00:24:52.219
Those costs can add up,

00:24:52.219 --> 00:24:53.500
so there's always vibes.

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:55.260
Just try it, see how it goes.

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:56.380
Maybe it's fine, I don't know.

00:24:57.339 --> 00:25:00.140
I'm joking, but honestly, like dogfooding,

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:01.500
really trying your own tools,

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:02.619
sit down with your agent

00:25:02.619 --> 00:25:04.219
after you've set up the agentic loop

00:25:04.219 --> 00:25:05.660
and really try it in earnest

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:06.780
and try and break it.

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:07.819
So you see what happens

00:25:07.819 --> 00:25:09.819
when you prompt inject it or whatever.

00:25:09.819 --> 00:25:11.180
You kind of want to get a feel

00:25:11.180 --> 00:25:13.020
for how that model behaves

00:25:13.579 --> 00:25:15.260
because that's what your users are going to see.

00:25:15.260 --> 00:25:17.660
Okay, I thought the idea of this slide

00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:19.739
was kind of like, what would we do differently

00:25:19.739 --> 00:25:20.780
if we were to start again?

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:22.540
But to be honest, that was this entire talk

00:25:22.540 --> 00:25:23.819
because we kind of did start again

00:25:23.819 --> 00:25:25.020
with Posit Assistant.

00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:27.579
We switched from a Microsoft

00:25:27.579 --> 00:25:28.939
copilot-based infrastructure

00:25:28.939 --> 00:25:31.099
to a more agentic workflow.

00:25:31.099 --> 00:25:33.339
So everything I've told you today already

00:25:33.339 --> 00:25:34.459
is kind of what we would do

00:25:34.459 --> 00:25:35.339
if we were starting again.

00:25:35.339 --> 00:25:36.859
But I will really quickly add,

00:25:36.859 --> 00:25:38.459
these are kind of my takeaways,

00:25:38.459 --> 00:25:40.380
but I will say this one's important.

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:42.300
Try to avoid adding complexity

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:43.020
to your agents.

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:45.020
Try and keep things as simple as possible

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:46.780
because you can do things

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.619
like prompt your agent

00:25:48.619 --> 00:25:50.300
to a mile away and back.

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:51.660
You can do things like set up

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.979
very complicated workflows.

00:25:53.979 --> 00:25:55.020
But what you will find

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:56.380
is this time next year,

00:25:56.380 --> 00:25:57.819
the models are five times as big.

00:25:57.819 --> 00:25:58.939
They're five times as clever.

00:25:58.939 --> 00:26:00.780
And all of that work that you spent

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:02.459
building those very long prompts,

00:26:02.459 --> 00:26:03.739
building those workflows

00:26:03.739 --> 00:26:04.939
are no longer needed.

00:26:04.939 --> 00:26:06.619
This is called the bitter lesson

00:26:06.619 --> 00:26:08.060
and it's been around a long time

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:09.260
before LLMs.

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:11.420
It's a very old AI idea

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:13.020
that don't work too hard

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:14.140
because the models

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:15.979
are going to get better anyway.

00:26:15.979 --> 00:26:17.420
So yeah, keep it simple.

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:18.380
Thanks very much.

